A||N||15||Cozmo||3800 install Questions||||5||
Z||000000||Cozmo||05-07-2001||09:30 AM||||Just got a '88 Olds with a excellent running 3800 in it for $200.  The body is toast but the engine is superb!  What would be required to put this engine/trans combo in my '86 auto GT?<BR>I've done a search but didn't find a concise answer.<BR>Thanks,<BR>C02M0||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||05-07-2001||10:28 AM||||Go to,<BR> <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com" TARGET=_blank>www.americancustomeng.com</A> <P>Take a look while your there.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||05-07-2001||11:50 AM||||Better, go to  <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||05-07-2001||01:32 PM||||Bob,<P>I've attached a couple of links that may give you a good idea of the details of the swap: <A HREF="http://www.terranexus.com/fieroman/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.terranexus.com/fieroman/</A>  <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html#Engine" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html#Engine</A>  Conversions, V-8s<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||3
Z||000004||Cozmo||05-08-2001||08:37 AM||||Thanks for the links!<BR>Couple of questions still...<BR>What motor and trans. mounts are needed? (keeping the 3800 trans.)<BR>Are special driveshafts required?<BR>Everything else looks pretty straightforward.<BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||05-08-2001||10:52 AM||||Bob,<P>I've got a couple of links to Pennock's topics that discuss using various OD transmissions. I don't know which one you have but, based on the face that it's an older model, it's probably a 440T. After you check the links, you may want to do a search. Try to find exactly which transmission you have and search on that for more specific info.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009240.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009240.html</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006479.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006479.html</A> <BR>Some of the older links I have don't work so, the above is all that I have. I know there are several topics on the 440T but, a search will have to be performed.<P>See at the meeting,<BR>Roy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||05-08-2001||03:59 PM||||Use the Oldsmobile trans steel brackets with Fiero rubber mounts, re-drill or slot the rear (trunk side) mount holes in the cradle. Move them toward the passenger side about an inch. The engine mount will have to be fabricated to your liking or purchased from one of the above companies. Axles need to be assembled using parts from various different GM vehicles.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cozmo||05-09-2001||08:17 AM||||Great! Thanks, all!<BR>I was going to go to a 4.x Caddy, but after some research decided it's too weird of an engine. I'm afraid it would be a "one year wonder" and repairs on it are so involved and parts are costly. The 3800 (I.E. 3.8, 231 C.I.) engine has a stellar reputation and the one I have runs great...good compression and vacuum etc. It is a VIN "C" engine (165 HP/210 torque) hooked to a 4T60 trans.<BR>I've found most of the mounting and axle info, just a couple of other questions... <BR>How about some suggestions on that pesky thermostat housing?<BR>Exhaust- use the existing outlet and do some "creative pipe bending" or weld in a down pipe on the crossover and patch it into a stock Fiero exhaust system?<BR>Remove the P/S pump (unless I can find one of them Fiero power steering racks <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).  Do I keep the A/C compressor that's on the engine and just mate it to the Fiero system? (A/C is a must in Florida with a black GT).<BR>Fuel pump- the Fiero is 45 PSI, the 3800 is 32 PSI. Do I swap pumps or get an adjustable regulator?<BR>I guess things like the front motor mount, belt/ pulley configuration, gearshift modification, DIS brick relocation etc. will be more apparent when I actually get to the "pile 'o parts" stage.  I'll be "turning wrenches" on this project in about a month and will continue gather as much data as I can.  If anyone has any suggestions or questions I'll be glad to share whatever I've been able to accumulate. I apologize for bringing up a subject that has been hashed over a gadzillion times, but there doesn't seem to be a concise list of mods, procedures and parts needed for this conversion anywhere... maybe this thread can become that...<BR><B>THANKS!</B><BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||05-09-2001||03:35 PM||||Don't do the swap. You will be severely disappointed. You will soon find that you took a well engineered 2.8L installation and turned it into a Frankenstein for a mere 20HP.<P>If you can't afford to do a 3.4L/3.1L (which leaves the installation essentially the same) or go to a motor with substantially more HP (200+), don't do the swap.<P>I'm serious. Leave the car alone. If you do this swap, you'll wish you hadn't.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-09-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000009||elfiero||05-09-2001||04:05 PM||||In regards to your fuel pump question,the<BR>fuel pressure regulator is built into the<BR>fuel rail of the engine this will control<BR>the pressure to 32psi it makes no difference<BR>how much the pump is capable of putting out.<BR>My engine(TPIV8)requires 60psi,and the stock<BR>V6 pump works just fine. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000010||westtexas||05-09-2001||09:24 PM||||Terryk, I have '87 2.5 auto with a blown engine I bought just to learn about swapping. I was planning on trying the 4.9 swap. The 3.8, to my inexperienced eyes, also seems reasonable. I would appreciate you explaining to newcomers like me the bad side of this swap. I also have a wrecked '85 with a V-6 auto that I could use for parts to build up a 3.4 to put in the '87. Your pros and cons will be appreciated. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||05-09-2001||10:17 PM||||I swapped an '87 Olds 3.8 into an '86 GT with 4 speed Muncie, and I was not at all disapointed. It doesn't compare to the 3800 SC or TPI 350 that I have driven, but it is still a big improvement over the 2.8. Considering the old 150 hp 3.8 can be had for literally nothing and the 200+ hp version costs about $1500 and up, the older engine can be very tempting. If I had a 150 hp 3.8 and a Fiero that needed an engine, I would not hesitate to do it again. But if you've got the cash for the better engine, go for the better one. Conversion costs are about the same.||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||05-10-2001||12:43 AM||||I guess I like things clean. You'll find that you are fixing little details for a long time. If you have a cheap engine and a car you can play with and not spend tons, sure go ahead. Great learning experience. <P>Done correctly, you will spend $1000 over the cost of the engine to make it look and drive like it belongs there. <P>But things that seemed fine before the swap all of the sudden aren't fine and you are adding more money to the car. <P>I know it's easy to encourage someone to do a swap, but frankly, unless you are gaining something substantial, I just don't think it's worth the effort or cost. <P>I've done three now and I wouldn't spend the time for anything less than 200HP. The 3.4L upgrade is certainly worth it though, but that's not a swap.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-10-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cozmo||05-10-2001||09:33 AM||||terryk, you must have ESP!!!<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>If you have a cheap engine and a car you can play with and not spend tons, sure go ahead. Great learning experience.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I got the GT for $500 with a bad engine and the donor for $200.  I'll do all the fab/mod stuff myself (my hobby/ relaxation). You're right, any swap is not for the faint of heart and the $$$ do add up.<BR>   I'm pretty new to Fieros (got my first one a year and a half ago)  but have done some pretty wild swaps and mods on other vehicles in the past and yes, weird problems I didn't anticipate popped up consistently.  <BR>   Some of my reasoning on this swap concerns the "modularity" of the GM FWD design. This is what allowed Pontiac to design the Fiero so cheaply in the first place.  Effectively you have a "propulsion pod" consisting of an engine and trans.  The most difficult part  is interfacing with the required subsystems. Cooling, axles, electrical, exhaust, A/C, mounting/ clearance etc.  If I can address these problems for the vin "C" 3800 than most of the solutions should apply to the newer higher horsepower 3800s also.  That way when I find that 3800SC with 94 miles sitting in a chicken coop covered with chicken s**t for $50 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I can drop it in with some confidence.<BR>Question:<BR>I've heard that for the 4T60 (440 T4) non-electronic tranny the original Fiero auto driveshafts will work as is. Truth?<BR><B>C02M0</B><P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||05-10-2001||02:07 PM||||Somewhere I have the overview of my 3800SC swap into my 86SE. Same car. I'll try to dig it out.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cozmo||05-11-2001||07:50 AM||||Thanks, terryk,<BR>   If you could post this info or point us to it, that would be <B>GREATLY</B> appreciated! <BR><B>C02M0</B> ||||reg||1
